WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.552
This video covers the general rules for
dressing cable.

2
00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:14.643
They come from DRS 01.00.01, General Fiber
Cable Dressing.

3
00:00:15.393 --> 00:00:19.617
Lesson 5B covers the tray and the front of the
rack.

4
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:28.021
We will cover the six rules, the standards
behind them, Velcro and bend radius, rated

5
00:00:28.021 --> 00:00:30.759
cable, and what to ask your lead.

6
00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:41.891
To dress a cable means to route and fasten it
so it hangs neatly, keeps a safe bend, and

7
00:00:41.891 --> 00:00:45.064
stays out of the way of other equipment.

8
00:00:46.464 --> 00:00:50.069
Four DRS sheets cover dressing so far.

9
00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:59.566
DRS 01.00.01 has the general rules for every
cable you dress.

10
00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:14.910
DRS 01.00.03 covers the basket tray,
01.00.03.01 vertical runs, and 01.00.02 the

11
00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.193
front of the rack.

12
00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:21.589
Those three are in lesson 5B.

13
00:01:22.339 --> 00:01:25.432
Have the sheets on hand when you dress cable.

14
00:01:26.782 --> 00:01:28.062
Rule one.

15
00:01:28.812 --> 00:01:41.214
Cables are dressed in accordance with three
industry standards: ANSI/TIA-568, TIA-606, and

16
00:01:41.214 --> 00:01:42.316
TIA-942.

17
00:01:43.666 --> 00:01:44.903
Rule two.

18
00:01:45.653 --> 00:01:50.624
Use half-inch hook and loop straps, which
everyone calls Velcro.

19
00:01:51.374 --> 00:01:54.126
Nylon zip ties are not allowed.

20
00:01:55.476 --> 00:01:56.692
Rule three.

21
00:01:57.442 --> 00:01:58.807
Bend radius.

22
00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:07.070
Follow the manufacturer's spec, with a minimum
of twenty times the cable diameter under

23
00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.437
tension, and ten times with no tension.

24
00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:13.110
Rule four.

25
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.804
Supports must not damage the cable.

26
00:02:17.554 --> 00:02:19.431
No metal staples.

27
00:02:20.781 --> 00:02:22.040
Rule five.

28
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:27.654
Support cable every four to five feet on
horizontal runs.

29
00:02:28.404 --> 00:02:34.569
The sheet gives this as the TIA-568
recommendation.

30
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:37.370
And rule six.

31
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:46.597
Use cable rated for the space: plenum, riser,
or low smoke zero halogen where local code

32
00:02:46.597 --> 00:02:47.464
requires.

33
00:02:51.614 --> 00:02:54.046
Rule one names three standards.

34
00:02:54.796 --> 00:02:59.767
You will not be handed these on site, but you
should know what each one covers.

35
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:10.336
TIA-568 is the standard for telecom cabling in
buildings: how cable is installed and

36
00:03:10.336 --> 00:03:11.428
supported.

37
00:03:12.178 --> 00:03:16.893
Rule five, support every four to five feet,
comes from it.

38
00:03:19.193 --> 00:03:27.235
TIA-606 is the administration standard:
identifiers, labels and records.

39
00:03:27.985 --> 00:03:32.188
Your label rules from lesson four are part of
that work.

40
00:03:33.538 --> 00:03:42.482
TIA-942 is the standard for data center
infrastructure, including cabling and pathways

41
00:03:42.482 --> 00:03:46.018
like the trays your cables run in.

42
00:03:47.368 --> 00:03:54.515
And the fire ratings in rule six come from the
NEC, the National Electrical Code.

43
00:03:55.865 --> 00:04:01.859
When you explain why a cable is dressed a
certain way, cite the DRS rule.

44
00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:10.353
For example: DRS 01.00.01, rule 2, Velcro
only.

45
00:04:14.503 --> 00:04:16.061
Back to rule two.

46
00:04:16.811 --> 00:04:18.197
Why Velcro?

47
00:04:18.947 --> 00:04:25.049
A zip tie pulled tight squeezes the cable and
can damage the fiber inside.

48
00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:29.191
Hook and loop holds the bundle without
crushing it.

49
00:04:30.541 --> 00:04:32.013
Now rule three.

50
00:04:32.763 --> 00:04:39.421
The bend radius is how tightly a cable is
allowed to curve, measured from the center of

51
00:04:39.421 --> 00:04:41.488
the curve out to the cable.

52
00:04:43.788 --> 00:04:50.093
When the cable is loaded, meaning under
tension, such as while you pull it, the

53
00:04:50.093 --> 00:04:53.047
minimum is twenty times its diameter.

54
00:04:54.397 --> 00:04:59.517
When it is unloaded, with no tension, the
minimum is ten times.

55
00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:04.216
The manufacturer's spec always applies too.

56
00:05:04.966 --> 00:05:08.827
If the spec gives a bigger number, use the
bigger number.

57
00:05:09.577 --> 00:05:20.610
DRS 01.00.03 says it again: keep the
manufacturer's recommended bend radius at all

58
00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:21.417
times.

59
00:05:22.767 --> 00:05:29.610
For example, a cable a quarter inch across
needs at least five inches of radius while you

60
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.839
pull it, and two and a half inches once it is
in place.

61
00:05:35.189 --> 00:05:41.717
Why it matters: a tight bend lets light leak
out of the fiber, and can crack the glass.

62
00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.473
You will not see the damage.

63
00:05:45.823 --> 00:05:47.807
One thing to ask about.

64
00:05:48.557 --> 00:05:54.423
The guide also recommends fifteen times for
long-term installed cable.

65
00:05:55.173 --> 00:06:09.616
That number is not on DRS 01.00.01, which
points to DRS 01.00.07 for fiber bend radius.

66
00:06:10.366 --> 00:06:13.502
Never go below the DRS minimums.

67
00:06:17.652 --> 00:06:21.684
Rule six is about where a cable is allowed to
run.

68
00:06:22.434 --> 00:06:26.679
Every cable jacket has a fire rating printed
on it.

69
00:06:27.879 --> 00:06:34.839
A plenum space is part of the building's
air-handling system, such as some spaces above

70
00:06:34.839 --> 00:06:35.559
ceilings.

71
00:06:36.309 --> 00:06:39.360
Smoke there would travel through the air
system.

72
00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:47.683
Only plenum-rated cable may run there: OFNP
for fiber, CMP for copper.

73
00:06:49.983 --> 00:06:53.610
A riser is a vertical path between floors.

74
00:06:54.360 --> 00:07:00.888
It needs riser-rated cable: OFNR for fiber,
CMR for copper.

75
00:07:02.238 --> 00:07:09.897
Where local code requires it, use LSZH, which
stands for low smoke zero halogen.

76
00:07:11.247 --> 00:07:16.943
A higher rating can replace a lower one, but
never the other way around.

77
00:07:17.693 --> 00:07:20.871
Plenum can go where riser is called for.

78
00:07:21.621 --> 00:07:24.757
Riser can never go in a plenum space.

79
00:07:26.107 --> 00:07:31.953
If the rating on the jacket does not match the
space, stop and ask your lead.

80
00:07:36.103 --> 00:07:38.449
Let us work out one example.

81
00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:43.573
Use your own cable's diameter and spec sheet
on the job.

82
00:07:44.323 --> 00:07:48.013
Say the cable is 0.4 inches across.

83
00:07:49.413 --> 00:07:56.347
While you pull it, it is loaded: twenty times
0.4 is eight inches of radius.

84
00:07:57.697 --> 00:08:04.289
Once it is in place with no tension: ten times
0.4 is four inches.

85
00:08:05.639 --> 00:08:11.761
If the spec sheet says five inches, five is
bigger than four, so you use five.

86
00:08:13.111 --> 00:08:17.079
And remember, the radius is half the width of
the loop.

87
00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:22.309
A five inch radius makes a loop about ten
inches across.

88
00:08:26.459 --> 00:08:32.091
First, Velcro sizes, because two sizes appear
in the documents.

89
00:08:33.091 --> 00:08:38.019
To bundle cables together, use half-inch
Velcro everywhere.

90
00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:49.457
Three DRS sheets say so, and DRS 01.00.03 says
half-inch for all cable bundling.

91
00:08:50.807 --> 00:08:56.759
Three-quarter inch Velcro may be used to
secure cable to a vertical tray.

92
00:08:57.509 --> 00:09:05.744
That is DRS 01.00.03.01, and the guide says
the same.

93
00:09:06.494 --> 00:09:09.865
Lesson 5B covers vertical runs.

94
00:09:11.215 --> 00:09:13.391
One question for your lead.

95
00:09:14.141 --> 00:09:19.367
The guide recommends fifteen times bend radius
for the long term.

96
00:09:20.117 --> 00:09:25.579
The DRS gives twenty and ten, and points to
another sheet for fiber.

97
00:09:26.929 --> 00:09:32.497
Where the guide and the DRS differ, the DRS is
the requirement.

98
00:09:33.247 --> 00:09:36.468
Follow it, and tell your lead about the
difference.

99
00:09:40.618 --> 00:09:47.638
In lesson five of the course, explore the six
rules, work out bend radius, match each

100
00:09:47.638 --> 00:09:51.519
requirement to its rule, and spot the
mistakes.

101
00:09:52.269 --> 00:09:59.395
Then take the lesson check, and go on to
lesson 5B: the tray and the front of the rack.

